# 4. The run is the provenance

PythonLab section 45 states an invariant that most systems fail:

> The graph must keep INTENDED, PLANNED, EXECUTED, OBSERVED, DERIVED, INFERRED
> and HYPOTHESIZED distinct. A requested temperature, a measured one, and a
> causal claim are three different things.

And section 25 requires provenance for everything, including failures.

The usual way to meet this is to instrument: run the protocol, emit log lines,
then parse the log back into a graph. Section 39 forbids the analogous move on
the input side -- there must not be a separate Semantic IR parser -- and for the
same reason it is worth not doing on the output side either. A log that is
re-parsed can disagree with the thing that produced it.

ndombolo does not have a logging step. The script **is** the graph, and the run
deposits its own record. This tutorial is about reading them.

## Set up something worth tracing

```turbulance
item OD_threshold = 0.40
item blank = 0.04

funxn corrected(raw):
    return raw - blank

item raw_a1 = 0.45
item raw_a2 = 0.42
item raw_a3 = 0.51

item od_a1 = corrected(raw_a1)
item od_a2 = corrected(raw_a2)
item od_a3 = corrected(raw_a3)

item total = od_a1 + od_a2 + od_a3
item mean_od = total / 3

print("mean corrected OD600", mean_od)
print("above threshold", mean_od > OD_threshold)
```

```output
mean corrected OD600 0.42
above threshold true

OD_threshold = 0.4
blank = 0.04
corrected = closure(corrected)
raw_a1 = 0.45
raw_a2 = 0.42
raw_a3 = 0.51
od_a1 = 0.41000000000000003
od_a2 = 0.38
od_a3 = 0.47000000000000003
total = 1.26
mean_od = 0.42
```

Three raw readings, a blank correction, a mean. PythonLab section 27 insists a
Measurement and a DerivedResult are different objects; here `raw_a1` is the
measurement and `mean_od` is derived, and the next two commands show that the
distinction survived.

## The graph, without running anything new

    ndombolo graph 04-the-run-is-the-provenance.ndo

It prints JSON with two arrays. `items` lists every name the document declares,
with its kind and the line it came from. `contacts` lists which name fed which,
with a weight counting how many times.

There is no extraction step here. The graph comes from one linear pass over the
source, which is why the source and the graph cannot disagree -- there is
nothing for them to disagree about.

Look for `raw_a1 -> od_a1` and `od_a1 -> total`. That chain is the derivation,
and it is exactly what a later reader needs in order to ask *where did this
number come from*.

## The trace: every event, in order

    ndombolo trace 04-the-run-is-the-provenance.ndo

The trace is the run rather than the text. Each event carries a sequence
number, a rule name, the line, what it read, and what it wrote.

The rules in this document are `declare-item`, `declare-funxn`, `return`,
`print` and `expr`. Later tutorials add `declare-motion`, `declare-proposition`,
`support`, `contradict`, `within`, `given`, `declare-point` and
`resolve-motion`.

There is no `call` rule. A funxn call surfaces as the `return` event at the
funxn's own line, carrying the arguments it read -- so the three calls to
`corrected` appear as three `return` events at line 27, each reading `blank`
and its own `raw`.

Two things to notice.

First, **reads are recorded by name and value together**. An event that read
`blank` records both the name and `0.04`. PythonLab section 24 asks execution
activities to record requested versus actual parameters; this is the same
information, one level down.

Second, `trace` **stops at the first failing cell** while `graph` does not.
That asymmetry is deliberate: the graph is a statement about the text, which is
readable whether or not it runs, while the trace is a statement about a run,
which genuinely stopped.

## A measurement that disagrees with its request

Section 24's example is a requested 37 degrees against an actual 36.82. Keeping
both is the whole point:

```turbulance
item requested = {temperature: 37, shaking: 700}
item actual = {temperature: 36.82, shaking: 698}

item drift_t = actual.temperature - requested.temperature
item drift_s = actual.shaking - requested.shaking

print("temperature drift", drift_t)
print("shaking drift", drift_s)
print("within half a degree", abs(drift_t) < 0.5)
```

```output
temperature drift -0.18
shaking drift -2
within half a degree true

requested = {temperature: 37, shaking: 700}
actual = {temperature: 36.82, shaking: 698}
drift_t = -0.17999999999999972
drift_s = -2
```

Neither map overwrites the other. Ask the trace what `drift_t` read and it
names both sources, which is the machine-readable form of *this number is a
discrepancy, not a measurement*.

## The record is not the output

Three different things sit beside this document, and telling them apart is most
of understanding what ndombolo guarantees.

The **output block** is a view of the last run. Clearing removes it.

The **trace** is the events of a run, regenerated each time you ask.

The **record** is a counter of deposits that only goes up.

    ndombolo record 04-the-run-is-the-provenance.ndo
    ndombolo clear  04-the-run-is-the-provenance.ndo
    ndombolo record 04-the-run-is-the-provenance.ndo

The `clear` reports how many output blocks it removed. The record does not
move -- and when you re-run, it advances past where it was rather than back to
it.

This is what makes the record provenance rather than state. A protocol you ran,
discarded the output of, and ran again is a protocol that was run twice, and
the record is the only one of the three that says so.

## What the trace cannot tell you

The trace answers every question of the form *what happened, and what did it
read*. It does not answer *which of these steps was load-bearing*.

Those are different questions. The trace is a complete record of the search --
every step the run actually took. Whether a given step was **necessary** to the
result is not a projection of that record, however finely you instrument it:
you would have to run the protocol without that step and compare.

So when you ask *did the blank correction matter*, the trace tells you that it
happened and what it read, and you are the one who has to remove it and look.
The document makes that cheap, because removing a cell and re-running is one
edit -- and the record will show both runs.

